    "notes": "Net-zero vs baseline (129/244 both). NOT a no-op: an A/B on the same 244 clips (features.js with correction vs a neutralized copy) shows the correction changes coefs on 27% of clips and flips 10 predictions -- 3 wrong->right, 3 right->wrong, 4 wrong->different-wrong. All 6 flips fire at the LAST 2-5 frames of the voiced span (spans as short as 26 frames), never in the stable middle -- e.g. jiao4 (win): frames 22-25/26 corrected -6..-12ST; shei2 (loss): frames 6-18/93 corrected up to +24ST via 2-pass amplification (an already-corrected neighbor biases the next passs local median, escalating a modest misjudgment past a full extra octave); er4 and nu3 (losses): exactly +12ST on the final 2 frames, structurally IDENTICAL to ta1 and one nu3 case that were wins. Root cause: a genuine tone-final low creak (T3/T4s defining low tail) and a genuine tracker octave-slip are LOCALLY INDISTINGUISHABLE to a pitch-only continuity check -- both are a low excursion bounded by higher neighbors. Next refinement to try: gate correction on HNR -- a true tracker error should keep periodicity comparable to neighbors (confident wrong-octave candidate), while genuine creak measurably degrades it. Also: cap correction to +-1 octave and use single-pass against ORIGINAL (not already-corrected) neighbors to remove the 2-pass amplification bug. Confirmed harmless (no Tone Perfect regression, no ToneAudio regression) but not yet a proven win -- ship/keep, do not build further conclusions on top of it until the HNR-gated version is validated."
  },
